1
00:00:01,600 --> 00:00:05,295
Go to sleep with issues. Wake up with pull requests.

2
00:00:05,475 --> 00:00:08,612
This is Shep: the night shift for your backlog.

3
00:00:09,992 --> 00:00:14,186
Coding agents are great. But today, you sit and watch them work.

4
00:00:14,366 --> 00:00:18,711
You babysit terminals, answer prompts, and re-run failing builds.

5
00:00:18,891 --> 00:00:22,458
Shep is built for the other moment: coming back to finished work.

6
00:00:23,837 --> 00:00:27,659
The idea is simple. In the evening, you hand Shep tasks.

7
00:00:27,839 --> 00:00:30,315
Each task moves through five states.

8
00:00:30,495 --> 00:00:35,443
Queued. Working. Then Ready, or Needs you. And finally, Merged.

9
00:00:35,623 --> 00:00:37,100
That's the whole product.

10
00:00:38,480 --> 00:00:42,151
Handing work over looks exactly like what you already do.

11
00:00:42,331 --> 00:00:46,594
In Linear or Jira, add the shep label, or move the ticket to Tonight.

12
00:00:46,775 --> 00:00:49,959
In Slack, react with a sheep on any message.

13
00:00:50,139 --> 00:00:55,784
On GitHub, label the issue. Or just type it: shep add, and a sentence.

14
00:00:55,964 --> 00:00:59,705
You can even tell your coding agent: give this to Shep for tonight.

15
00:01:01,085 --> 00:01:03,456
Shep answers right where you asked.

16
00:01:03,636 --> 00:01:08,074
Got it. I'll work on this tonight at eleven. No new inbox to check.

17
00:01:09,454 --> 00:01:11,616
At eleven, the shift starts.

18
00:01:11,796 --> 00:01:20,820
Your own coding agent, like Claude Code or Codex, works each task in its own copy of the code, so your work in progress is never touched.

19
00:01:21,000 --> 00:01:27,748
The sky is the progress bar. It starts at midnight blue, and warms toward dawn as each task finishes.

20
00:01:27,928 --> 00:01:31,286
Every finished task is a sheep walking into the pen.

21
00:01:32,666 --> 00:01:36,361
If a build fails, Shep reads the log and fixes it.

22
00:01:36,541 --> 00:01:41,002
If a task is unclear, it asks one question, and moves on to the next.

23
00:01:41,181 --> 00:01:42,716
And it never wakes you up.

24
00:01:44,097 --> 00:01:49,104
Curious at two in the morning? The Dynamic Island shows the shift at a glance.

25
00:01:49,284 --> 00:01:51,910
Or open the terminal, and watch the sky.

26
00:01:53,290 --> 00:01:57,379
In the morning, one notification: five ready, one needs you.

27
00:01:57,559 --> 00:02:03,204
Open the review window. Every pull request is a draft that already passes its checks.

28
00:02:03,384 --> 00:02:08,774
Proof comes first: a screenshot of the change, and a second opinion from a reviewer agent.

29
00:02:10,154 --> 00:02:15,068
You decide with one key. M to merge. S to send it back, with a note.

30
00:02:15,248 --> 00:02:19,070
Shep never merges on its own. You always press the button.

31
00:02:20,450 --> 00:02:26,919
The answer also lands where the task came from. The Slack thread gets the pull request, and a Merge button.

32
00:02:27,099 --> 00:02:29,133
The ticket moves to In review.

33
00:02:29,314 --> 00:02:33,473
Your teammates see it happen. That's how Shep spreads through a team.

34
00:02:34,853 --> 00:02:37,712
Clear the queue, and the sky reaches full dawn.

35
00:02:37,892 --> 00:02:41,424
That's your morning: five minutes, not five hours.

36
00:02:42,804 --> 00:02:45,245
Getting started takes about ten minutes.

37
00:02:45,425 --> 00:02:48,783
Install the Mac app, or run one command in the terminal.

38
00:02:50,163 --> 00:02:53,521
Shep starts by asking where your tasks come from today.

39
00:02:53,701 --> 00:02:59,880
Linear, Jira, Notion, Monday, Slack, GitHub, or nowhere in particular.

40
00:03:00,060 --> 00:03:03,731
Connect them with a click. Your tokens stay in your Mac's keychain.

41
00:03:03,911 --> 00:03:08,836
Then it shows the moves you'll use to hand work over. The same ones you make today.

42
00:03:10,217 --> 00:03:13,029
Pick your coding agent, and your repository.

43
00:03:13,209 --> 00:03:15,336
Choose when the shift starts.

44
00:03:15,517 --> 00:03:21,975
Shep then finds good candidates in your tools: small, clear tasks. It flags the vague ones.

45
00:03:22,154 --> 00:03:27,834
Hand three over for tonight. Or try one right now, and watch your first pull request appear.

46
00:03:29,214 --> 00:03:33,025
Shep lives in four places, and they all show the same thing.

47
00:03:33,205 --> 00:03:36,273
The terminal, with four commands and a split view.

48
00:03:36,452 --> 00:03:37,558
The Mac app.

49
00:03:37,738 --> 00:03:39,343
Inside your coding agent.

50
00:03:39,523 --> 00:03:42,556
And inside the tools your team already uses.

51
00:03:43,936 --> 00:03:45,924
The look is night turning into morning.

52
00:03:46,104 --> 00:03:50,484
A lantern for the brand. A dithered pixel sky, and warm amber light.

53
00:03:50,664 --> 00:03:54,742
Sheep stand for tasks. Calm at night. Bright when the work is done.

54
00:03:56,121 --> 00:03:58,666
Shep. Dependabot for your backlog.

55
00:03:58,846 --> 00:04:02,494
Go to sleep with issues. Wake up with pull requests.
